--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  TRAINING DATA PROVENANCE — DISCLOSURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This Section is disclosure, not restriction. It is here so You can make Your own
assessment before building on the Model.

4.1  The Model was trained from scratch on the Source Dataset — a corpus of
     approximately 1,960 clips segmented from roughly 412 videos across 50 raag
     classes, collected from publicly accessible internet sources, principally
     video-sharing platforms. Training used the corpus's 1,810-clip training
     split; the remaining 150 clips are the held-out test set.

4.2  The Author does not own the recordings in the Source Dataset and did not
     obtain permission from their rightsholders. The Source Dataset is
     distributed under its own terms, which grant no licence in those
     recordings and restrict redistribution of them. If You intend to retrain,
     reproduce or extend this work using that corpus, read those terms first.

4.3  The following characteristics of the Model bear on how it relates to its
     training material, and are stated for that reason:

     (a)  The Model is DISCRIMINATIVE, not generative. It maps audio and a
          tonic frequency to a probability distribution over 50 raag classes.
          It has no decoder, no vocoder and no synthesis capability, and cannot
          reproduce or reconstruct any training recording.

     (b)  The CQT branch is a small 2-D residual network of approximately
          554,000 parameters trained on roughly 1,810 clips. The melody branch
          is logistic regression over a 120-bin pitch histogram expressed in
          cents above the tonic. Neither is designed to memorise training
          audio, and at this architecture and scale memorisation sufficient to
          recover a recording is very unlikely — though the Author has not run
          a memorisation or extraction study and does not assert the point as
          established. The melody branch's input representation is
          non-invertible by construction: a folded pitch histogram discards
          time, timbre, dynamics and phase, and no recording can be recovered
          from it.

     (c)  What the Model encodes is the interval structure of raags — which
          scale degrees occur, with what relative weight, above Sa. A raag as
          such is a traditional musical framework of considerable antiquity,
          held in common, and is not anyone's copyrighted property. Particular
          compositions in a raag — a bandish, an arrangement, a notated
          realisation — may well be protected, as is every recording; the raag
          in the abstract is not.

4.4  The Author's understanding, offered as context and not as a legal opinion,
     is that a small discriminative classifier sits differently from a
     generative model trained on the same material, and differently again from
     the corpus itself. In Bartz v. Anthropic PBC, No. 3:24-cv-05417 (N.D. Cal.
     June 23, 2025), Judge Alsup held that training large language models on
     lawfully acquired copyrighted books was "exceedingly transformative" and a
     fair use, and that format-shifting lawfully purchased books was too, while
     downloading and retaining pirated copies to build a permanent central
     library was not — and could not be cured by transformative use downstream.

     Three qualifications matter and are stated because they cut against the
     Author's own interest. First, the training holding rested on lawfully
     acquired copies; the acquisition step is judged separately, and it is the
     acquisition step that is unlicensed here. Second, Bartz concerned
     generative language models and says nothing about audio classifiers, so
     applying it to a model of this kind is extrapolation. Third, it is one
     unreviewed district court decision: it was followed by a USD 1.5 billion
     settlement, granted final approval in July 2026, rather than by appellate
     review, and it binds no one outside that court.

4.5  Nothing in this Section is a representation that the Model is free of
     third-party claims. See Section 7.


